// WQL — A VALUE THAT CANNOT SURVIVE THE EL'S i64 NORMALIZATION IS REFUSED, NOT
// REINTERPRETED.
//
// The EL's scalar baseline is i64, so a UDF returning a narrower integer is
// widened at the emit site with `((f(x)) as i64)`. For u64/usize that cast
// REINTERPRETS and for u128/i128 it TRUNCATES — the number the query goes on to
// use is not the number the function returned. That was written down as
// "documented MVP semantics" in a doc comment and was a silently wrong row.
//
// ⚠ WHAT IT MEASURED. `order by ukey(a.v)` with `fn ukey(i64) -> u64` over the
// values 1, 2^64-1, 5, 2^63 emitted `__ks.push(((ukey(a.v)) as i64))` and returned
// them in the order 2^63, 2^64-1, 1, 5. The comparison is TOTAL — the axis the
// join-order licence used to test — and every carried nest agreed on it, so no
// plan decision could have detected it and the trace said nothing. The control in
// the same run, an identical query with a `u32` return, answered correctly: the
// axis is whether the VALUE SURVIVES the widening, not signedness.
//
// ⚠ REFUSED AT THE CALL SITE (`codegen::check_calls`) AND NOT AT THE SORT. The
// normalization is not the sort's: the same cast feeds `select`, `where`,
// `group by` and an aggregate argument, and a per-clause check would have to be
// written four more times and would still leave the fifth. One use-site refusal
// closes all of them — including the `select` case here, which is a wrong ROW and
// not merely a wrong order.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;
use logos.lang.option;

struct A { pub k: i64, pub v: i64 }

pub fn ukey(v: i64) -> u64 { return v as u64; }

pub deem qu(as_: &[A]) {
    from as_ a
    select ukey(a.v) }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let as_: [A; 1] = [ A { k: 1i64, v: -1i64 } ];
    let g: Vec<i64> = qu(&as_[..]).unwrap();
    return g.len() as i32;
}
